.SH HISTORICAL NOTE
Screen mode interface of \fBvash\fR was implemented with a \fItermcap\fR-based \fIlinlib\fR library. This library was thoughts inspired by some project from CERN
then used for real-time applications in 1980x.
Later, library was extended with some tools and named \fIvisi\fR.
.PP
Initially \fBvash\fR was wrote on 16-bit environment on
DEC PDP-11/23 and VT100 hardware,
using classic K&R dialect of C language.
Only ASCII-based 8-bit encodings were supported.
.PP
1st stricted release was about 1990 year under
name \fIash\fR, assistant shell. 
Further development was episodical.
In 1991-1993 years ash was ported to i386 platforms ISC UNIX and FreeBSD,
then UnixWare. Some enhancements was added with port to SPARK Solaris in 1995 and DEC OSF-1 Unix in 1996. Porting to SGI IRIX was done in 1997.
.PP
1st port to linux-based systems was done in 2004 since Fedora Core 2.
.PP
In modern time project development was reactivated not observing comparable
tools, then prepared to wide publication under free license.
.PP
Finally, name \fIvash\fR was assigned to this program avoiding confusion with /bin/ash, \fIagain shell\fR.
.PP
Most of previouse versions was for 32-bit architectures and
supported only 8-bit encoding charsets.
Native 64-bit support added since 2019.
Probably, 16-bit version may be still compiled, not sure.
.PP
\fITermcap\fR-based code was modified to use \fIterminfo\fR.
Starting in 2020, UTF-8 support was implemented 
but only for restricted set of languages 
including european and chinessy. 
.PP
Interface of vash itself remains english only.
